"Combines descending category counts with cumulative percentage to reveal how contributions concentrate.\n\nUse when: Nonnegative additive counts can identify the largest contributors. Readers need to decide how many categories account for a stated share.\n\nAvoid when: Values are negative, overlapping, or have incomparable denominators.\n\nVisual grammar: Zero-based bar lengths encode category counts in descending order. A cumulative line encodes the running fraction of the overall total.\n\nBuild: Aggregate counts by mutually exclusive categories and sort descending. Compute cumulative totals divided by the complete included total. Draw bars and a clearly labeled 0–100% cumulative axis, marking any chosen threshold.\n\nCheck: Check the cumulative sequence never decreases and ends at 100%. Verify the displayed categories cover the declared denominator. Confirm both axes are labeled and the line is identified as cumulative.\n\nPitfalls: Two axes are acceptable only because their different roles are explicit. An 80% reference is a heuristic, not a law implied by this chart.\n\nExample: Rank synthetic support causes by ticket count and mark where their cumulative share first reaches 80%.\n\nAlternatives: Bar chart, ECDF, Stacked bar chart."
